WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:17.520
<v Ira Glass>OK. Imagine for second what it would be like to have the entire country have one opinion about something and you were the only person who disagreed. Out of the entire country. This pretty much happened to the woman I'm about to introduce you to. She was a juror in the criminal trial of Rod Blagojevich.
<v Ira Glass>好的。试想一下，如果全国上下对某件事都只有一种看法，而你是唯一持反对意见的人。全全国上下只有你一个。我接下来要向大家介绍的那位女性，就几乎遇到了这种情况。她是罗德·布拉戈耶维奇刑事审判案中的一名陪审员。

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:29.570
<v Ira Glass>You may remember Blagojevich, Governor of Illinois. When Barack Obama became president, Blagojevich was caught on tape talking on the phone about how he might use Obama's senate seat for his own personal profit.
<v Ira Glass>你可能还记得布拉戈耶维奇，伊利诺伊州前州长。当贝拉克·奥巴马当选总统时，布拉戈耶维奇在电话录音中被抓到谈论他将如何利用奥巴马空出的参议院席位来谋取个人私利。

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:38.280
<v Rod Blagojevich>I've got thing, and it's [BLEEP] golden. And I'm just not giving it up for [BLEEP] nothing.
<v Rod Blagojevich>我手里有个好东西，简直是[消音]绝佳的宝贝。我绝不可能白白[消音]把它拱手让人。

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:50.400
<v Ira Glass>When the tapes became public, Blagojevich did the kind of media blitz that did not seem to win many people over. He squirmed on talk shows, he appeared as a contestant on The Apprentice. Here he is about to get fired by Donald Trump.
<v Ira Glass>这些录音公之于众后，布拉戈耶维奇展开了一场媒体公关狂潮，但似乎并没有赢得多少人的好感。他在脱口秀节目中局促不安，还作为参赛选手上了《学徒》节目。这里是他即将被唐纳德·特朗普解雇的画面。

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.560
<v Donald Trump>Your Harry Potter facts were not accurate. Who did the research?
<v Donald Trump>你关于哈利·波特的细节并不准确。是谁做的调研？

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:04.880
<v Rod Blagojevich>There was not a specific direction to do the research on Harry Potter, but the inability to learn the product-- and it was an issue in the--
<v Rod Blagojevich>虽然没有明确的指令去对哈利·波特进行调研，但无法掌握产品信息——而且这是一个关于——

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.100
<v Donald Trump>Was what their inability or your inability?
<v Donald Trump>那是他们无法掌握，还是你无法掌握？

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.660
<v Rod Blagojevich>I suppose I should have directed everybody else to learn the product.
<v Rod Blagojevich>我想我本应该指导其他人去掌握这些产品信息。

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:37.750
<v Ira Glass>And so most of us who saw him on TV decided, "that guy is guilty." And then the case went to trial. Seven weeks. Jury deliberated for 14 days after that. Six men, six women. 24 counts against the governor. The jury could only agree on one count-- lying to the FBI. They convicted him on that one. It was split, with a handful of not guilty votes in various configurations, on the 23 other counts.
<v Ira Glass>因此，我们大多数在电视上看到他的人都断定：“这家伙有罪。”然后案件进入了审判阶段。持续了七周。在此之后，陪审团商议了14天。六男六女。针对州长的24项指控。陪审团仅就一项指控达成一致——向联邦调查局撒谎。他们判定他这一项罪名成立。在其余23项指控上，陪审团意见分歧，有少数几张无罪票，组合情况各不相同。

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:55.090
<v Ira Glass>And on the charge that everybody was the most interested in, selling the senate seat, that was the one charge where the vote was 11 to 1. 11 to 1. The holdout? A 67 year old grandmother, JoAnn Chiakulas, who says that yes, the jury did know that the people of America wanted this guy convicted.
<v Ira Glass>而在人们最感兴趣的那项指控——兜售参议院席位上，唯独这项指控的投票结果是11比1。11比1。那个坚持己见的孤立者是谁呢？是一位67岁的祖母，名叫乔安·恰库拉斯（JoAnn Chiakulas），她说，是的，陪审团确实知道美国人民希望这个人被判有罪。

00:01:55.090 --> 00:02:28.490
<v Joann Chiakulas>Well, I think that that was a big part of it. I think that there was a tremendous amount of pressure on the jurors to convict, and I do think a lot of it had to do with people didn't like him. He was an annoyance, he was on TV. And so I think it was like people wanted to make him go away. I mean, I certainly had that feeling. And I think that those of us who had some doubts felt a great deal of pressure.
<v Joann Chiakulas>嗯，我认为这是很大一部分原因。我认为陪审团承受着巨大的定罪压力，而且我认为很大程度上是因为人们不喜欢他。他是个讨厌鬼，总上电视。所以我认为就像是人们想让他彻底消失一样。我是说，我确实有这种感觉。而且我认为我们当中有些疑虑的人承受了巨大的压力。

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:50.070
<v Joann Chiakulas>It was probably one of the most difficult things I've experienced in life. I couldn't sleep at night. I had headaches, I had stomachaches.
<v Joann Chiakulas>[掌声]

00:02:50.070 --> 00:03:01.610
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio show, we have three stories of people who end up as the lone holdout, the last man standing, in various situations. And if these three stories are any indication, it is always pretty dreadful.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们有三个关于在各种情况下最终成为孤立的少数、坚持到最后的人的故事。如果这三个故事有什么共同预示的话，那就是这总是相当可怕的。

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:06.870
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《此间生活》，由公共广播国际（Public Radio International）发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:24.870
<v Ira Glass>In JoAnn's case, she says that she's been on a couple of juries before, and nothing like this happened. She went into the case with no particular fondness for Blagojevich and no desire to draw attention to herself. In fact, it was kind of the opposite on that score. In the aftermath of the trial, hounded by the press for weeks, she did one interview. I was her second.
<v Ira Glass>就乔安的情况而言，她说她以前当过几次陪审团成员，从来没有遇到过这样的事。她参与这个案件时，对布拉戈耶维奇并没有什么好感，也没有想过要吸引别人的注意。事实上，在这方面恰好相反。审判结束后，她被媒体围追堵截了好几个星期，她只接受了一次采访。我是她的第二个采访对象。

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:45.390
<v Ira Glass>She says she simply felt that the prosecution failed to make its case. When she looked at the entirety of the secret tape recordings of Blagojevich, he said so many scattered different things about what he would do with that senate seat-- maybe he'd give it to Oprah, maybe he'd take it himself, maybe he'd leverage it into something else-- it just didn't seem to rise to the level of a criminal conspiracy, which was the charge against him.
<v Ira Glass>她说她只是觉得控方没有充分举证。当她查看布拉戈耶维奇的所有秘密录音时，他说过太多零散且不同的话，关于他将如何处理那个参议院席位——也许他会把它给奥普拉，也许他会自己留着，也许他会把它当作筹码换取别的东西——这根本达不到刑事共谋的程度，而这正是对他提出的指控。

00:03:45.390 --> 00:04:06.650
<v Joann Chiakulas>There were enough other things in the transcripts that could make one think that this guy was, like, a little nuts and just talking. Just talking off the top of his head.
<v Joann Chiakulas>文字记录中还有足够多的其他内容，让人觉得这家伙就像有点疯了，只是在胡说八道。完全是信口开河。

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:13.820
<v Ira Glass>Do you remember when you got your first hint that you were seeing the case differently than some of the other people on the jury?
<v Ira Glass>你还记得是什么时候第一次察觉到你看待这个案件的视角和陪审团里其他一些人不一样吗？

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:58.820
<v Joann Chiakulas>Probably almost as soon as we were beginning our deliberations. I think when we came back into the room, as people started to talk, there were some people who were very firm in their belief that he was guilty, and there were some other people who were not so certain of that. I think what happened as we went along is that the people who felt he was guilty did not look at anything to the contrary. I got a sense that there was no sense that there might be reasonable doubt or there could be reasonable doubt. So whenever anyone in the room raised an issue that might have questioned the guilty verdict, those that felt that he was guilty were very antagonistic, very hostile, very critical, very demeaning.
<v Joann Chiakulas>大概几乎在我们开始商议时就开始了。我想当我们回到房间里，大家开始讨论时，有些人非常坚定地认为他有罪，而另一些人则没有那么确定。我想随着讨论的深入，那些认为他有罪的人根本不去看任何相反的证据。我有一种感觉，他们压根不觉得这里面可能存在合理的怀疑，或者可能存在合理的怀疑。所以每当房间里有人提出可能会对有罪判决产生质疑的问题时，那些认为他有罪的人就表现得非常具有对抗性、非常敌意、非常批判、非常贬低。

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:24.060
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you, does some particular example come to mind of some time when you or somebody else raised one of these points and the reaction you got from the side that thought that he was guilty?
<v Ira Glass>我能问一下，有没有什么具体的例子让你印象深刻？比如你或其他人提出这些观点时，那些认为他有罪的人给你的反应？

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:55.980
<v Joann Chiakulas>Well, I think that some of us felt that some of the material was open to interpretation as to what the governor meant when he said certain things. And it was often the women who had more reasonable doubt then the men. Most of the men on the jury felt that the defendant was guilty. We were criticized.
<v Joann Chiakulas>嗯，我认为我们中的一些人觉得，对于州长在说某些话时的真正意图，部分材料是开放式的，可以有不同的解读。而且往往是女性比男性有更多的合理怀疑。陪审团里的大多数男性都认为被告有罪。我们遭到了批评。

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:23.970
<v Joann Chiakulas>And it was so difficult in the room sometimes that one juror cried several times because she felt that her point of view was not being listened to. We were made to feel that we were not smart enough, we weren't looking at the evidence correctly, we were misinterpreting the facts.
<v Joann Chiakulas>有时房间里的气氛是如此艰难，以至于有一位陪审员哭了好几次，因为她觉得自己的观点没有被倾听。他们让我们觉得自己不够聪明，没有正确看待证据，在曲解事实。

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:28.420
<v Ira Glass>You're saying that the men were saying things that were demeaning. What kinds of things did they say?
<v Ira Glass>你是说男人们说了一些贬低人的话。他们说了些什么样的话？

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:54.550
<v Joann Chiakulas>Well, I can't recall specifically, but it was a dismissive attitude. "Oh, you're not looking at the facts." "We have to convict him, because the prosecution will have to retry the case if we don't." Or, we'll be embarrassed if we don't find him guilty."
<v Joann Chiakulas>嗯，我记不清具体的话了，但那是一种轻蔑的态度。“哦，你们没有看事实。”“我们必须判他有罪，因为如果我们不这样做，控方就得重审此案。”或者，如果我们不判他有罪，我们会感到尴尬。”

00:06:54.550 --> 00:07:01.030
<v Ira Glass>Hey JoAnn, are there any other moments for you that stand out when you were deliberating?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，乔安，在你们商议期间，还有没有其他让你记忆犹新的时刻？

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:49.940
<v Joann Chiakulas>One gentleman who sat somewhere else decided that he had to sit across from me so that he could look into my eyes. And I felt it was very much an act of intimidation. I think that one day in particular towards the end-- I think we had been there for 12, 13 days at that point-- and I think that was the day the gentleman sat across for me, and another man said he wanted to tell the judge that one of the jurors was not deliberating in good faith. I really felt that I was going to lose it that day because of the pressure.
<v Joann Chiakulas>有一个坐在其他地方的男士决定坐在我对面，这样他就能直视我的眼睛。我觉得这在很大程度上是一种恐吓行为。我记得特别是在接近尾声的一天——我想当时我们在那里已经待了12、13天了——我想就是那个男士坐在我对面那天，另一个男人说他想告诉法官，其中一位陪审员没有本着善意进行商议。我真的觉得那天我快要崩溃了，因为压力太大了。

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:53.800
<v Ira Glass>What happened after the verdicts were announced? What happened to you then?
<v Ira Glass>宣判之后发生了什么？你当时怎么样了？

00:07:53.800 --> 00:08:21.040
<v Joann Chiakulas>Well, we were cautioned by the judge to not give any statement and to take a few days to think about how we wanted to approach it. And we all agreed that we were not going to talk about who voted how, and that we were all going to go home and not give a statement. So we all left thinking that that was going to be the case.
<v Joann Chiakulas>嗯，法官曾警告我们不要发表任何声明，花几天时间考虑一下我们想如何处理这件事。我们都同意不谈论谁投了什么票，我们都回家去，不发表任何声明。所以我们离开时都以为事情会是这样的。

00:08:21.040 --> 00:09:00.060
<v Joann Chiakulas>But I could not go home, because I received a phone call after I got off the train that there were swarms of reporters in front of my house. And so I went straight to my daughter's. And at that point, the phone started ringing nonstop and went on for days, if not weeks. We turned on the TV, and we saw some of the jurors talking immediately. They were pointing to the fact that there was one juror who was the holdout, and the press figured out that it was me.
<v Joann Chiakulas>但我没能回家，因为我下了火车后接到一个电话，说我家门前聚集了大量的记者。所以我直接去了我女儿家。从那时起，电话铃声开始响个不停，持续了好几天，甚至好几周。我们打开电视，看到一些陪审员立刻开始接受采访。他们指出有一个陪审员是持异议的孤立者，媒体很快查出那个人就是我。

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:30.600
<v Joann Chiakulas>There was a helicopter flying over my daughter's house, which is where I was saying. One gentleman on TV, who was a reporter, said that he had listened to the evidence and he definitely thought that Blagojevich was guilty. And the only way that he could explain the fact that I voted not guilty was because I was crazy. So it was absolutely overwhelming. I don't think it stopped for at least a month.
<v Joann Chiakulas>有一架直升机盘旋在我女儿家房子上空，也就是我当时住的地方。电视上有一个男记者说，他听了证据，绝对认为布拉戈耶维奇是有罪的。而他能解释我投无罪票的唯一原因，就是我疯了。所以这绝对是压倒性的。我想这种情况至少持续了一个月才停下来。

00:09:30.600 --> 00:10:13.650
<v Joann Chiakulas>And I think that it-- I don't think, I know that it really made me question jury service in general in terms of, if you go in and you do your duty as a citizen, you're vilified if you do not give the verdict that people want and expect. If a person who serves on a jury knows that they are going to be vilified, harassed after the fact, maybe that'll have an effect on how they act in the jury room. And that's kind of chilling to me.
<v Joann Chiakulas>而且我认为这——我不只是认为，我知道这确实让我对陪审团制度本身的意义产生了质疑：如果你参与其中并尽到了公民的职责，但如果你给出的不是人们想要和期待的判决，你就会遭到恶意中伤。如果一个担任陪审员的人知道他们在事后会遭到恶意中伤和骚扰，这或许会对他们在陪审室里的行为产生影响。这让我感到有点不寒而栗。

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:44.780
<v Joann Chiakulas>Let's say we're talking about a death penalty case. And people did call me and tell me that they know of people-- and one person in particular served on a case where it was a death penalty case-- they went into the jury room thinking that they had reasonable doubt, but based on the pressure that they felt in the jury room they voted guilty. And have regretted it ever since.
<v Joann Chiakulas>假设我们谈论的是一个死刑案。确实有人打电话告诉我，他们知道有人——其中一个人特别在一个死刑案中担任陪审员——他们走进陪审室时心里觉得有合理的怀疑，但基于他们在陪审室里感受到的压力，他们投了有罪票。并且为此后悔至今。

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:45.900
<v Ira Glass>JoAnn Chiakulas.
<v Ira Glass>乔安·恰库拉斯。

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:59.000
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "DO YOUR DUTY" BY CANDI STATON]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 -坎迪·斯塔顿的《尽你的职责》]

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:03.570
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Last Man Stand-Up.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，孤军脱口秀。

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:13.700
<v Ira Glass>Most people do not choose to end up as a last man standing. It just kind of happens to them. Sarah Koenig has a story about that somebody like that, somebody who was simply following his dream.
<v Ira Glass>大多数人并不想成为那个坚持到最后的人。这只是某种程度上降临在他们头上。萨拉·科尼格（Sarah Koenig）有一个关于这样的人的故事，一个只是在追随自己梦想的人。

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:31.410
<v Sarah Koenig>Duke Fightmaster was making good money, but he hated his job. More than hated it-- he loathed it. Felt like he was dying inside. He was a mortgage broker. He was married with two sons-- a toddler and a baby-- living in Orange County, south of LA. Then the mortgage crisis happened, his company collapsed. So he got another job.
<v Sarah Koenig>杜克·法特马斯特（Duke Fightmaster）当时赚得不错，但他讨厌自己的工作。不仅仅是讨厌——他痛恨这份工作。觉得自己内心正在枯死。他是一名按揭贷款经纪人。他已婚，有两个儿子——一个蹒跚学步，另一个还是婴儿——住在洛杉矶以南的奥兰治县。后来抵押贷款危机爆发了，他的公司倒闭了。于是他又找了一份工作。

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:50.670
<v Duke Fightmaster>Working in a cubicle doing, like, phone sales for people in credit card debt. This really depressing office-- dark with, like, eight cubicles in it. And you're just on the phone all day calling people that are deciding whether to file for bankruptcy.
<v Duke Fightmaster>在隔间里工作，比如给身陷信用卡债务的人打电话做电话销售。这是一个非常令人沮丧的办公室——黑乎乎的，里面大约有八个隔间。你整天都在打电话，打给那些正在决定是否申请破产的人。

00:11:50.670 --> 00:12:07.630
<v Duke Fightmaster>And I was just thinking, "This is so depressing." And I had this idea that if I just follow my passion or find something that I'm passionate about, something that uses my creativity, and if I just am able to find that and then throw myself into it, I'll be successful.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我当时就在想，“这太让人沮丧了。”然后我萌生了一个想法：如果我只是追随自己的激情，或者找到一个让我充满激情的东西，一个能运用我创造力的事情，如果我能找到那个东西并全身心地投入其中，我就会成功。

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:28.280
<v Sarah Koenig>His two initial ideas, in Duke's own opinion, were stupid. He would start a boy band, or else he would make Orange County, which has a lot of churches, into a huge Christian rock scene, like Seattle was for grunge. Actually, these ideas aren't quite as stupid as they might sound if you know that Duke had once had a semi-successful rock band, which toured college towns all over California.
<v Sarah Koenig>在杜克自己的看法中，他最初的两个想法简直蠢透了。他要组建一个男孩乐队，或者把教堂很多的奥兰治县变成一个巨大的基督教摇滚圣地，就像西雅图对于垃圾摇滚那样。事实上，如果你知道杜克曾经组建过一个半成功的摇滚乐队，并在全加州的大学城巡演过，你就会发现这些想法其实并没有听起来那么愚蠢。

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:35.990
<v Sarah Koenig>So he started in on both these projects but soon realized he was interested neither in boy bands, nor Christians. It was 2007.
<v Sarah Koenig>于是他开始着手这两个项目，但很快发现自己既对男孩乐队不感兴趣，对基督徒也没兴趣。那是2007年。

00:12:35.990 --> 00:13:00.980
<v Duke Fightmaster>And then right around that time I was watching TV and I saw that Jay Leno was going to step down and Conan O'Brien was going to replace Jay Leno, and so they needed someone to replace Conan O'Brien. And then the light just clicked on in my head-- "Why don't I just do a talk show?" It just felt like it clicked. I said, "I'll start a talk show. I'll just do it in my bedroom."
<v Duke Fightmaster>就在那个时候，我在看电视，看到杰·雷诺（Jay Leno）要卸任了，柯南·奥布莱恩（Conan O'Brien）将接替杰·雷诺，所以他们需要有人来接替柯南·奥布莱恩。然后我的脑子里灵光一闪——“我为什么不自己做一个脱口秀呢？”感觉就是一拍即合。我说：“我要创办一个脱口秀节目。我就在我的卧室里做。”

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:15.650
<v Duke Fightmaster Show Announcer>Good evening, ladies and gentlemen. Recorded somewhere in the US Southland, it's the Duke Fightmaster Show. Tonight on Duke's show, special audience member, Robert, the comedy stylings of Robert, and special guest, Robert.
<v Duke Fightmaster Show Announcer>晚上好，女士们先生们。录制于美国南方某处，这里是《杜克·法特马斯特秀》。今晚杜克节目的特邀观众，罗伯特；罗伯特带来的喜剧风格；以及特邀嘉宾，罗伯特。

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:38.070
<v Sarah Koenig>Duke Fightmaster is not a man of half measures, and he believed in the aphorisms of success, that if he wanted something badly enough, he would have it. He was going to replace Conan O'Brien. Not by doing all the normal things like getting a production job at a local talk show, learning the business, working his way up. Instead, he would make himself into a talk show sensation from his own bedroom.
<v Sarah Koenig>杜克·法特马斯特不是一个半途而废的人，他坚信成功的格言，认为如果他极度渴望某样东西，就一定能得到它。他要取代柯南·奥布莱恩。他不是通过所有常规途径，比如在当地脱口秀节目中获得一个制作工作、了解这个行业、从底层一步步干起。相反，他要从自己的卧室里把自己打造成一个脱口秀巨星。

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:57.440
<v Duke Fightmaster>And so I had my little desk and I arranged it, and I arranged the couch for the guests to sit. And I had these little curtains that went out to a deck, and I just stood in front of them. And I had a video camera, so I had my friend hold the video camera. And we just started to-- we just did a show.
<v Duke Fightmaster>所以我有了我的小桌子并把它布置好，我还布置了供嘉宾坐的沙发。我挂了一些通往露台的小窗帘，然后我就站在它们前面。我有一台摄像机，所以我让我的朋友帮我拿着摄像机。然后我们就开始——我们就做了一档节目。

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:06.910
<v Duke On Stage>It's good to be here. I feel alive right now. I'm finally doing it. This is my own talk show, I'm stoked, I'm going all the way.
<v Duke On Stage>很高兴来到这里。我现在感觉自己充满了活力。我终于在做了。这是我自己的脱口秀，我太兴奋了，我要一干到底。

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:19.850
<v Sarah Koenig>That's Duke's friend Tanya's French bulldog yapping in the background. Duke is standing in front of a white curtain, actually his bedroom drapes. He's a little chubby, he's got wavy brown hair. He's wearing a black sweater and jeans.
<v Sarah Koenig>背景里传来的是杜克的朋友塔尼亚（Tanya）的法国斗牛犬的叫声。杜克正站在一幅白色的窗帘前，实际上那是他卧室的窗帘。他有点胖，留着波浪状的棕色头发。他穿着一件黑色毛衣和牛仔裤。

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:31.070
<v Sarah Koenig>The camera is jerking around like crazy, and when it swings over to the audience, you see a few people in chairs. Someone else is sitting on the bed, which is covered in satiny pillows. Everyone looks happy and a little confused.
<v Sarah Koenig>摄像机疯狂地晃动着，当镜头摇向观众席时，你可以看到几个人坐在椅子上。另一个人坐在铺着缎面枕头的床上。每个人看起来都很开心，又有点困惑。

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:40.380
<v Duke On Stage>I'm in a relationship, been in a relationship for 10 years. And my secret to a long lasting relationship is, don't leave.
<v Duke On Stage>我在恋爱中，已经谈了10年恋爱了。我长久恋爱的秘诀是：别离开。

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:56.660
<v Duke Fightmaster>I would do a little monologue, then I would talk to my wife, who was my sidekick. And then my best friend was my second sidekick. He was like an Andy Kaufman and he'd do something weird and crazy. Then I'd interview a guest and then we'd have a little music act, and we'd do it all in like seven minutes.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我会做一段简短的开场独白，然后和我妻子聊天，她是我的搭档。接着我最好的朋友成为我的第二搭档。他就像个安迪·考夫曼（Andy Kaufman），会做一些古怪疯狂的事情。然后我会采访一位嘉宾，接着我们有一个小型音乐表演，这一切都在大约七分钟内完成。

00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:00.340
<v Duke On Stage>Now it's time for our first guest, Robert.
<v Duke On Stage>现在是时候迎来我们的第一位嘉宾，罗伯特。

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:05.380
<v Duke On Stage>[APPLAUSE]
<v Duke On Stage>[掌声]

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:06.880
<v Duke On Stage>Robert, how are you doing this evening?
<v Duke On Stage>罗伯特，你今晚过得怎么样？

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:10.270
<v Robert>Good, good. Do you guys have any water or anything?
<v Robert>好，好。你们有水什么的吗？

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:11.690
<v Leslie>You can have my water.
<v Leslie>你可以喝我的水。

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:31.470
<v Duke Fightmaster>And I remember I put it out on YouTube, and I remember I kept checking how many hits it was. And by the next episode it was like, I told everyone it was 72 hits, but then I pretty much figured out that most of those were me just clicking on it and just checking it again what the hits were.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我记得我把它发到了YouTube上，我记得我一直在查看它有多少点击量。到了下一集的时候，就像是，我告诉所有人有72次点击，但随后我基本上发现，其中大部分其实都是我自己点进去又再次查看点击量。

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:35.580
<v Sarah Koenig>So every time you clicked again, were you like, wow, another person has looked since I last looked.
<v Sarah Koenig>所以每次你再次点击时，你心里都在想，哇，自我上次查看以来，又有一个人看了。

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:45.350
<v Duke Fightmaster>Exactly, yes. That's what happened.
<v Duke Fightmaster>完全正确，是的。事情就是这样的。

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:56.070
<v Sarah Koenig>The Duke Fightmaster Show got longer over time, to about 40 minutes, and developed regular characters. Besides Duke's wife Leslie and his best friend Ryan, he did one hilarious bit about declaring war on vegetables.
<v Sarah Koenig>随着时间推移，《杜克·法特马斯特秀》变得越来越长，大约有40分钟，并且发展出了固定的角色。除了杜克的妻子莱斯利（Leslie）和他最好的朋友瑞安（Ryan），他还表演了一个关于向蔬菜宣战的爆笑片段。

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.910
<v Ryan>No longer shall they take our land.
<v Ryan>它们再也不能占领我们的土地了。

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:00.750
<v Sarah Koenig>Ryan is actually really, really funny.
<v Sarah Koenig>瑞安其实真的非常非常搞笑。

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:05.290
<v Ryan>No longer shall they rape our women!
<v Ryan>它们再也不能强暴我们的妇女了！

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:12.310
<v Sarah Koenig>There was a beautiful, busty blond named Tantalizing Tanya. They had a house band, a number one fan, and a weekly catch phrase.
<v Sarah Koenig>这里有一个身材丰满、金发碧眼的漂亮尤物，名叫“迷人的谭雅”。他们有一支驻场乐队、一个头号粉丝，还有一个每周固定的口头禅。

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:16.240
<v Duke Fightmaster>There's no audience crazier than the Duke Fightmaster Show audience.
<v Duke Fightmaster>没有哪个观众比“杜克·费特马斯特秀”的观众更疯狂了。

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:18.610
<v Duke On Stage>There is no audience crazier than the Duke Fightmaster Show audience.
<v Duke On Stage>没有哪个观众比“杜克·费特马斯特秀”的观众更疯狂了。

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:21.500
<v Duke On Stage>[APPLAUSE]
<v Duke On Stage>[掌声]

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:44.230
<v Duke Fightmaster>The first show, I had two friends in the audience that I basically just forced to be my studio audience. And then in the second show I had maybe four people. And then it doubled to eight. And when I got to, like, episode 24, I had like 30 people packed into my bedroom.
<v Duke Fightmaster>第一期节目时，我有两个朋友在观众席里，基本上是我硬拉来充当我的演播室观众的。然后在第二期节目里，我大概有了四个人。接着人数翻倍到了八个人。当我做到大概第 24 期节目时，我的卧室里已经挤了大约 30 个人。

00:16:44.230 --> 00:17:04.630
<v Duke On Stage>It's tax week, it was tax week. And I actually do my taxes and take my annual blood test at the same time each year. So I have some good news-- I'm getting $2,000 back, and I have herpes. I'm sorry that I have to tell you this way, honey.
<v Duke On Stage>那是报税周，当时正好是报税周。我实际上每年都在同一时间报税并做年度血液检查。所以我有一些好消息——我要退税 2,000 美元，而且我得了疱疹。亲爱的，我很抱歉我得用这种方式告诉你。

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:06.930
<v Leslie>Don't be sorry. It's not like I'm going to get-- we don't really have--
<v Leslie>别抱歉。又不是说我要得——我们其实也没有——

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:21.560
<v Sarah Koenig>Episode 24 was key. That was the night a local reporter was in the audience. By that time, the show was looking more like a show. They had real graphics, and theme music. And a surprisingly big number of people were checking it out on YouTube every week.
<v Sarah Koenig>第 24 期节目是关键。那天晚上，观众席里坐着一位当地记者。到了那个时候，这个节目看起来更像个正经节目了。他们有了真正的视觉包装和主题曲。而且每周都有数量惊人的观众在 YouTube 上观看。

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:26.350
<v Duke On Stage>180,637 views.
<v Duke On Stage>180,637 次观看。

00:17:26.350 --> 00:17:28.440
<v Duke On Stage>[APPLAUSE]
<v Duke On Stage>别拉近，诺尔吉。好吗？我们待会儿再拍这个。该死的，诺尔吉。嘿，诺尔吉，你最近怎么样？哦，我忘了：我们根本不在乎。

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:39.590
<v Sarah Koenig>Fans and critics were writing in, so Duke started a segment on the show called, It's Time For the Comments. And he's wearing a suit now. His hair is cut, he's lost weight, he looks good. He made it into the newspaper.
<v Sarah Koenig>粉丝和评论家们纷纷写信来，于是杜克在节目中开辟了一个名为“是时候看评论了”的环节。他现在穿上西装了。他理了发，瘦了，看起来状态不错。他还登上了报纸。

00:17:39.590 --> 00:18:05.500
<v Duke Fightmaster>And then the following week we were on the front page of the paper in town. And then I got a couple other newspaper articles, and it just felt to me like something was happening. And other people actually really believed it so much that it almost seems like it could work. Like, maybe, who knows? Something could happen.
<v Duke Fightmaster>然后接下来的那一周，我们登上了镇上报纸的头版。接着我又上了其他几家报纸的报道，我当时就觉得好像有什么事情正在发生。而且其他人也 realmente 坚信这一点，以至于它看起来几乎像是有成功的可能。就像，也许，谁知道呢？可能会发生点什么。

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:20.080
<v Sarah Koenig>What was the plan, though? What was the long term plan? How is it going to go from a bunch of people you sort of half knew in your bedroom to actually going on TV? Or was the TV part just a lark, like you knew that was never going to happen?
<v Sarah Koenig>不过，当时的计划是什么？长期计划是什么？你要怎么从一群你半熟不熟的人在你的卧室里录节目，跨越到真正上电视？还是说上电视这部分只是个恶作剧般的玩笑，因为你明知那绝不可能发生？

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:52.940
<v Duke Fightmaster>You know, I was like, OK, we're going to be the replacement for Conan O'Brien. And I somehow thought that maybe I would pick up some type of esteem and become some internet sensation. And who knows, maybe someone would see me and go, wow, look how much this guy can do with no money. Why don't we just give him a chance. And it always seemed like one more person would find out about it, and it just seemed like success was just right around the corner.
<v Duke Fightmaster>你知道的，我当时想，好吧，我们要成为柯南·奥布莱恩的接班人了。我不知怎么地觉得，也许我会获得某种声望，成为某种互联网轰动人物。谁知道呢，也许会有人看到我然后说，哇，看看这家伙用零资金能做出多少成果。我们为什么不给他一个机会呢。而且似乎总会有更多人发现这个节目，感觉成功就近在咫尺。

00:18:52.940 --> 00:19:14.680
<v Sarah Koenig>Duke got a producer and an executive producer, and they started booking better guests-- actual working comedians, for instance, and popular local bands. Duke started producing commercials in hopes of getting sponsors. And he met weekly with a successful business friend to map out a plan to buy space on cable TV. By this time, Duke had left his job at the credit card place, so he needed the show to make some money.
<v Sarah Koenig>杜克找了一位制作人和一位执行制作人，他们开始预定更好的嘉宾——比如真正职业的喜剧演员，以及受欢迎的当地乐队。杜克开始制作商业广告，希望能拉到赞助商。他每周与一位成功的商业朋友会面，规划购买有线电视播出时段的方案。此时，杜克已经辞去了信用卡公司的工作，所以他需要节目能赚点钱。

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:35.570
<v Duke Fightmaster>I said, OK, well, I can quit my job and not work for three months. I have enough money to make this happen for three months. But then three months came, and after that I was like, wow, we got on the paper and we got all this, and I was just like, I can just go a little bit longer, just a few more episodes. And it was always just a few more episodes.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我说，好吧，我可以辞职，三个月不工作。我有足够的钱让这一切维持三个月。但后来三个月到了，在那之后我想，哇，我们上了报纸，我们得到了这一切，我当时就觉得，我可以再撑一会儿，再多做几期节目。结果总是“再多做几期节目”。

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:37.650
<v Sarah Koenig>At what point did you quit your job?
<v Sarah Koenig>你是在哪个时间点辞职的？

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:39.520
<v Duke Fightmaster>After the first episode.
<v Duke Fightmaster>第一期节目之后。

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:42.330
<v Sarah Koenig>Are you serious? You quit after the first episode?
<v Sarah Koenig>你认真的？你第一集播完就辞职了？

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:58.490
<v Duke Fightmaster>Yes. After the first episode, I was at my job watching the episode over and over, checking for the views, and then I quit. And then on the second episode, I told my wife that I quit my job.
<v Duke Fightmaster>对。第一集播完后，我在工作时一遍又一遍地看那一集，查看播放量，然后我就辞职了。然后在第二集的时候，我告诉了我妻子我辞职了。

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:00.140
<v Duke On Stage>I quit my job today.
<v Duke On Stage>我今天把工作辞了。

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:01.410
<v Leslie>Wait. What?
<v Leslie>等等。什么？

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:05.410
<v Duke On Stage>I quit my job. [APPLAUSE]
<v Duke On Stage>我把工作辞了。[掌声]

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:08.390
<v Duke On Stage>I'm no longer doing the debt settlement anymore.
<v Duke On Stage>我再也不用做债务协商了。

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:13.420
<v Leslie>You know, we have two kids and a lot of bills to pay. We'll talk about this later.
<v Leslie>你知道的，我们有两个孩子，还有一堆账单要付。我们待会儿再谈这个。

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:14.370
<v Duke On Stage>OK.
<v Duke On Stage>好吧。

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:34.870
<v Sarah Koenig>He had actually told Leslie the day before, and she was fine with it-- a three month experiment seemed reasonable. Soon after episode 24, the one that got them the press coverage, Duke had to move the show out of his bedroom. The neighbors were getting mad. One guy had written him a note that said, "Please stop whatever you're doing." He thought Duke was holding weight watchers meetings in the house, what with all that clapping.
<v Sarah Koenig>他其实在前一天就告诉莱斯利了，而且她觉得没问题——为期三个月的实验听起来还算合理。在第 24 期节目后不久——也就是给他们带来媒体报道的那期节目，杜克不得不把节目搬出他的卧室。邻居们开始生气了。有个家伙给他留了张字条，上面写着：“请停止你在做的任何事。”他以为杜克在家里开减肥互助会呢，因为总有那么多的鼓掌声。

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:46.650
<v Sarah Koenig>Duke found another venue, at the local veteran's hall, but it was a lot more work than the bedroom. Setting up and breaking down the set, plus the tech got more complicated-- more cameras. Duke start stressing out about it. He wasn't having as much fun.
<v Sarah Koenig>杜克在当地的退伍军人会馆找到了另一个场地，但那比在卧室里要费劲得多。搭建和拆卸布景，再加上技术方面变得更加复杂——摄像头更多了。杜克开始为此感到压力巨大。他没那么开心了。

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:58.740
<v Duke Fightmaster>I would be looking at the cameraman going, hey, he's not filming what he's supposed to be filming. And then I would start yelling at the cameraman, like, Norge, don't be zooming in on the camera-- you're not supposed to zoom.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我会看着摄像师说，嘿，他拍的不是他该拍的内容。然后我就会开始对摄像师大喊大叫，比如，诺尔吉，别把镜头拉近——你不该用变焦。

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:11.170
<v Duke On Stage>Don't zoom in, Norge. OK? We're going to get this later. Goddammit, Norge. Hey Norge, how are you doing? Oh, I forgot: we don't care.
<v Duke On Stage>别放大，诺吉。好吗？我们待会儿再弄这个。操，诺吉。嘿，诺吉，你怎么样？哦，我忘了：我们才不在乎。

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:20.880
<v Sarah Koenig>At this point there were about 20 people helping Duke put on the show every week, volunteering. And these volunteers were constantly backing out of shows of the last minute or just not showing up.
<v Sarah Koenig>此时大约有 20 人每周志愿帮助杜克制作节目。而这些志愿者经常在最后一分钟临阵退缩，或者干这就是爽约不来。

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:41.030
<v Duke On Stage>This is the story of my life. My right hand man's not here, flaking on me. Everyone lets me down. Ooh, I have a little baby boy, I need to watch him sleep. DJ Flux isn't here. And who else has bailed on me? None other than Chipper Starkenberg. He's bailed. He's bailed.
<v Duke On Stage>这就是我人生的写照。我的得力助手不在，放我鸽子。每个人都让我失望。哦，我有个小婴儿，我需要看他睡觉。DJ 弗拉克斯不在。还有谁放了我鸽子？不是别人，正是奇珀·斯塔肯伯格。他鸽了。他鸽了。

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:52.540
<v Sarah Koenig>Meanwhile, Duke's wife Leslie, who is a yoga teacher, was supporting the family. On the show, she's constantly letting him know she's not so happy about that. Her delivery is so deadpan it's hard to tell whether she's joking.
<v Sarah Koenig>与此同时，杜克的妻子莱斯利是一名瑜伽老师，一直在养家糊口。在节目中，她不断地让他知道她对此很不高兴。她的语气极其冷面幽默，让人很难分清她是在开玩笑。

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:59.200
<v Leslie>Duke, that voice isn't in your head. That's actually me asking, when are you going to get a job?
<v Leslie>杜克，那个声音可不在你脑子里。那其实是我在问，你到底什么时候去找工作？

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:01.250
<v Duke Fightmaster>That became her character: get a job.
<v Duke Fightmaster>那就成了她的标志性人设：去找工作。

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:02.360
<v Sarah Koenig>Here's another one.
<v Sarah Koenig>还有一个。

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:05.270
<v Duke On Stage>Now, who are these sponsors, you ask?
<v Duke On Stage>那么，你可能会问，这些赞助商都是谁呢？

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:10.020
<v Leslie>Yes. Who are these sponsors? Because I'd really love to quit my third job.
<v Leslie>对啊。这些赞助商都是谁？因为我真的很想辞掉我的第三份工作。

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:12.030
<v Sarah Koenig>And another one.
<v Sarah Koenig>又来一个。

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:13.730
<v Duke On Stage>How are you doing this evening? Pretty good?
<v Duke On Stage>今晚过得怎么样？挺好的？

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:16.890
<v Leslie>I'm a little tired because I have to work so much to support the family.
<v Leslie>我有点累，因为我必须拼命工作来养家糊口。

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:34.710
<v Duke Fightmaster>I think she was actually being sincere. We didn't have any direct communication unless the cameras were rolling. That's probably her being just direct with me. Get a job. I'm sick of this-- get a job. Only we had an audience laughing.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我觉得她当时其实是认真的。除非摄像机开着，否则我们之间没有任何直接沟通。那可能只是她对我很直接地表达。去找工作。我受够了这一切——去找工作。只不过我们旁边有一群观众在笑。

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:41.020
<v Sarah Koenig>And so was that the only time you were hearing it from her, though, was in front of the camera?
<v Sarah Koenig>所以，那是她对你说这番话的唯一场合吗？也就是在镜头前？

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:45.320
<v Duke Fightmaster>Yeah. That's when she was able to speak her mind.
<v Duke Fightmaster>对。那是她能够畅所欲言的时候。

00:22:45.320 --> 00:23:13.010
<v Leslie>I don't know. I kind of thought that I was pressuring him to get a job, but I guess maybe I wasn't pressuring him enough, if he thinks that I wasn't. But yeah, I mean, I think I was. I think toward the end, I said to him, "I can't think of any other place you're going to be able to live rent free other than your mom's house, so either you get a job or you're going to have to go find somewhere else to live rent free." I thought that was pretty direct. I don't know.
<v Leslie>我不知道。我以前以为我是在向他施压让他去找工作，但我想也许我给他的压力还不够，如果他觉得我没怎么施压的话。但对，我的意思是，我觉得我确实给过。我想到了最后，我对他说：“除了你妈家，我想象不出还有什么地方能让你白住，所以要么你去找份工作，要么你就得去别的地方找个能白住的地方。”我觉得这已经够直接了。我不知道。

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:19.240
<v Sarah Koenig>Wow. Were you sort of thinking, OK, well, any day now he's going to give this up?
<v Sarah Koenig>哇。你当时是不是在想，好吧，反正他随时都可能放弃这个？

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:24.290
<v Leslie>Yeah, I was thinking more and more that he wasn't going to give it up.
<v Leslie>对，我越来越觉得他不会放弃了。

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:25.120
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, really?
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，真的吗？

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:40.890
<v Leslie>Yeah. So more and more I was telling them that, "This is what we make, this is what our bills are, this is how much we're short every month." So his was idea was, "Well, let's get a roommate."
<v Leslie>对。所以我越来越经常对他们说：“这就是我们的收入，这就是我们的账单，这就是我们每个月资金短缺的数额。”所以他的主意是，“嗯，那我们找个室友吧。”

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:42.870
<v Sarah Koenig>He literally suggested a roommate?
<v Sarah Koenig>他真的建议找个室友？

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:55.790
<v Leslie>Yeah. I mean, that was his solution. Instead of him working, he would prefer to have a roommate move in.
<v Leslie>对。我是说，这就是他的解决方案。与其让他去工作，他宁愿让一个室友搬进来。

00:23:55.790 --> 00:24:12.080
<v Sarah Koenig>Duke's talk show had inched over the line from ambitious lark to obsession. And like all obsessions, it was greedy. It sucked up not only Duke's time and energy and temper, it sucked up money. So the family's finances weren't just suffering, they were a disaster.
<v Sarah Koenig>杜克的脱口秀节目已经越界，从一个雄心勃勃的恶作剧变成了一种痴迷。而像所有的痴迷一样，它是贪婪的。它不仅吞噬了杜克的时间、精力和脾气，还吞噬了金钱。所以这个家庭的财务状况不仅是在恶化，简直是一场灾难。

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:23.790
<v Duke Fightmaster>We had to leave our home that was kind of nice, in Laguna Beach, and we moved to where I am now in Dana Point. It was like half the rent.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我们不得不离开我们在拉古纳海滩那个还算不错的家，搬到了我现在所在的达纳波因特。那里的房租直接减半了。

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:33.570
<v Leslie>And you know, it got pretty scary here and there. Just, how are we going to pay for food? How are we going to buy clothes for the kids? You know, things like that.
<v Leslie>你知道的，这期间时常会让人感到相当害怕。就是，我们该怎么支付伙食费？怎么给孩子们买衣服？你知道的，诸如此类的事情。

00:24:33.570 --> 00:24:40.960
<v Duke Fightmaster>My car was repossessed. My home went into foreclosure.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我的车被扣押了。我的房子面临止赎。

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:55.980
<v Leslie>And we would have to borrow money from our families. And we had a house in San Francisco that we ended up short-selling because we couldn't afford to pay the mortgage on it anymore with the taxes. And so we lost our house in San Francisco.
<v Leslie>我们不得不向家人借钱。我们在旧金山有一套房子，最后不得不亏本出售，因为我们要付税，实在无力支付房贷了。所以我们失去了旧金山的房子。

00:24:55.980 --> 00:25:04.120
<v Duke Fightmaster>I maxed-out all my credit cards-- I think we had, like, maybe eight-- and our family went bankrupt.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我刷爆了我所有的信用卡——我想我们大概有八张左右——然后我们家破产了。

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:08.540
<v Sarah Koenig>But didn't you used to be one of those guys who counselled how to get rid of your credit card debt?
<v Sarah Koenig>但你以前不是那种专门提供如何摆脱信用卡债务咨询的人吗？

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:13.410
<v Duke Fightmaster>Yeah, yeah. That's-- well, I was just becoming one of my clients, basically.
<v Duke Fightmaster>对，对。那是——好吧，我基本上是变成了我自己的客户之一。

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:41.930
<v Leslie>So I was kind of hoping that he would look around him and see what was going on, and notice that he had two young children that needed to be taken care of. I don't know. I think there's kind of a fine line between having denial and have a good attitude. I think sometimes I probably tried to keep a positive attitude more then I should have.
<v Leslie>所以我当时其实是希望他能环顾四周，看看发生了什么，注意到他有两个年幼的孩子需要照顾。我不知道。我觉得保持逃避现实和保持良好心态之间有一条微妙的界限。我觉得有时候我可能比自己应该做的更努力地保持积极的心态。

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:50.570
<v Sarah Koenig>Duke was under so much pressure you could almost see it ricocheting through him on screen. Take episode 60. Duke's monologue is so fraught it's painful to watch.
<v Sarah Koenig>杜克承受的压力太大了，你甚至能看到它在屏幕上通过他整个人反射出来。看看第 60 期节目。杜克的独白紧绷得让人不忍直视。

00:25:50.570 --> 00:26:16.030
<v Duke On Stage>But, hey, that's what credit cards are for-- to help you in a time of need. So I'd use the credit cards-- Oh my God, I don't know what I'm doing. This is just getting very stressful. But you know, we're married now. And then my wife says, "My biological clock is ticking--"
<v Duke On Stage>但是，嘿，这就是信用卡存在的意义——在你需要的时候帮助你。所以我用信用卡——哦天哪，我不知道自己在干什么。这实在是压力太大了。但你知道的，我们现在结婚了。然后我妻子说：“我的生物钟在滴答作响——”

00:26:16.030 --> 00:26:33.220
<v Sarah Koenig>So this goes on for another five minutes or so. I can hear your breathing is getting a little weird. Leslie is looking a little concerned-- there are couple cuts to her and she looks kind of-- her brow is a little furrowed there. Just explain what was going on.
<v Sarah Koenig>所以这种情况又持续了大约五分钟。我能听到你的呼吸声变得有点奇怪。莱斯利看起来有点担心——镜头切到她好几次，她看起来有点——她的眉头有点紧锁。解释一下当时发生了什么吧。

00:26:33.220 --> 00:27:01.000
<v Duke Fightmaster>Yeah, well, I think that that particular night, the guests backed out, too. And all my-- I mean, all together I had probably had like 10 monologue writers who had all quit by that point. Just because they just had other stuff in their life. And so I was down to writing it myself. I think that was, like, one of my first ones that I wrote by myself.
<v Duke Fightmaster>对，嗯，我觉得那天晚上特别不巧，嘉宾也放鸽子了。而且我所有的——我是说，总共我大概有 10 个独白编剧，到那个时候都已经全部辞职了。纯粹是因为他们生活中有其他事情要忙。所以我只能自己写。我想那是，我独立撰写的首批独白之一。

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:29.080
<v Duke Fightmaster>And I was having panic attacks, like every day. They would last like, I don't know, like 20 minutes. But then I would be scared that I'd have another one. And then they'd last for weeks at a time, and it got to the point where I think I was having panic attacks for like six months in a row. And I think at that point right there, I wrote a monologue wanting to talk about stress, and then I started having panic attacks right then.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我当时几乎每天都在经历恐慌发作。它们每次持续的时间，我不知道，大约 20 分钟吧。但我又会害怕下一次发作。然后它们会持续好几周，甚至发展到我觉得自己连续六个月都在经历恐慌发作的地步。我觉得就在那个时候，我写了一段想谈论压力的独白，然后我当场就开始恐慌发作了。

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:30.390
<v Sarah Koenig>During the monologue?
<v Sarah Koenig>在独白期间？

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:33.300
<v Duke Fightmaster>During my monologue.
<v Duke Fightmaster>在我独白的时候。

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:53.500
<v Sarah Koenig>God. I don't understand why you didn't just stop. It sounds so miserable. Like, you were having panic attacks. You know, you're not having fun. Why didn't you just stop?
<v Sarah Koenig>天哪。我不明白你为什么不停下来。听起来太痛苦了。比如，你在经历恐慌发作。你知道的，你一点都不快乐。你为什么不停下来？

00:27:53.500 --> 00:28:15.270
<v Duke Fightmaster>I guess somewhere in my mind I just thought, you know, no matter how bad things get, I'll just kind of put my head down and keep going. And I knew that it was ridiculous, starting a talk show. I mean, I knew that, outwardly, people think I'm ridiculous.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我想在我的某个潜意识里，我只是觉得，不管情况变得多么糟糕，我都会埋头坚持下去。而且我很清楚，办一脱口秀节目是多么荒谬。我是说，我很清楚，在表面上，别人会觉得我很荒谬。

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:44.400
<v Duke Fightmaster>And I remember just driving around like, "The show isn't going anywhere, I'm not going anywhere, I've wasted these last years." And I remember just driving around so depressed. And I just felt like I had broken-- and I had a breakdown where I just started crying in my car. And I just felt like I hit the core of my being and it was, "you're a loser." And I think that was part of my rock bottom.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我记得自己当时开车到处转，心想：“这节目毫无起色，我也毫无进展，我把这几年都白白浪费了。”我记得自己开车时沮丧无比。我感觉自己彻底崩溃了——我在车里崩溃大哭。我感觉自己触及了内心的最底层，那就是：“你是个失败者。”我想那就是我跌入人生谷底的一部分。

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:45.720
<v Sarah Koenig>Did you quit after that?
<v Sarah Koenig>在那之后你辞职了吗？

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:55.140
<v Duke Fightmaster>No. No, I think I still went for another year after that.
<v Duke Fightmaster>没有。没有，我觉得在那之后我又坚持了一年。

00:28:55.140 --> 00:29:24.730
<v Duke Fightmaster>You know, anyone who makes it in this life at anything, you always hear, has to go through hell. So I figured, "I'll just go through hell." I remember my friend who worked in real estate worked for one of those cheesy real estate motivators that used to yell out, "You have to have a break down to have a break through." So I was thinking, "OK, I've had my break down, so now I'm going to actually break through to some new level."
<v Duke Fightmaster>你知道的，生活中做成任何事的人，你总会听说，都必须经历地狱般的磨难。所以我当时想：“那我就经历地狱好了。”我还记得我的一个做房地产的朋友，他曾为那些老套的房地产励志讲师工作，那些人总是大喊：“你必须经历崩溃，才能迎来突破。”所以我当时想，“好吧，我已经崩溃过了，所以现在我终于要突破到一个新高度了。”

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:41.220
<v Sarah Koenig>By episode 60 and the on-air panic attack, a year had gone by. Conan O'Brien, of course, had long been replaced by Jimmy Fallon. Duke still wasn't working, and the show still wasn't making any money. None of the sponsors materialized, though Duke did manage to get Red Bull to send them free drinks every week.
<v Sarah Koenig>到了第 60 期节目以及那次直播中的恐慌发作时，一年已经过去了。当然，吉米·法伦早就取代了柯南·奥布莱恩。杜克依然没有工作，节目也依然不赚钱。没有任何赞助商出现，不过杜克确实设法让红牛每周给他们免费寄饮料。

00:29:41.220 --> 00:30:11.870
<v Duke Fightmaster>The first people to leave were the monologue writers. And my best friend Ryan, he just got busier with work and had a baby. And the house band, Evan had left. And the other house band guy left. Our two camera people left, and I had to switch them. And I think I was down to like one camera guy, and then I was just getting random people who showed up at the show to operate cameras.
<v Duke Fightmaster>最先离开的是独白编剧。我最好的朋友瑞恩，因为工作越来越忙还有了孩子，也离开了。驻场乐队的埃文离开了。另一个驻场乐队的人也走了。我们的两个摄像师走了，我不得不换人。我想我当时只剩一个摄像师了，然后我只能随便找现场出现的人来操作摄像机。

00:30:11.870 --> 00:30:38.770
<v Duke Fightmaster>Leslie was basically not going to show that much. I think Tanya quit towards the end. I mean, towards the very end I was pretty much by myself. We had a number one fan, and he was always excited when he was there, but, I mean, they just eventually didn't want to commit every Friday night to me.
<v Duke Fightmaster>莱斯利基本上不太会出镜了。我觉得谭雅在快结束时也辞职了。我是说，到了非常接近尾声的时候，基本上只剩我一个人了。我们有一个头号粉丝，他在的时候总是很兴奋，但是，我是说，他们最终还是不愿意把每个周五晚上都承诺给我。

00:30:38.770 --> 00:31:10.240
<v Duke On Stage>Now, I have some news, and this could be our last night here at the Veteran's Hall. Unfortunately, these veterans, they want rent, is what they want. And these veterans, I mean, they just get so crazy. It's like, they're all up in your face and stuff just after only one time, just one time it happens, one time you don't pay rent for like four months, and then they call you and they're like, "Where's the rent?"
<v Duke On Stage>现在，我有一些消息，这可能是我们在退伍军人会馆的最后一个夜晚了。不幸的是，这些退伍军人，他们想要的是房租。这些退伍军人，我是说，他们真的太疯狂了。就好像，他们会在你刚经历了一次之后就当面质问你，哪怕只有一次，只要有一次你大概四个月没交房租，然后他们就会打电话给你，对你喊：“房租呢？”

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:13.300
<v Sarah Koenig>And did you, in fact, get kicked out of the Veteran's Hall?
<v Sarah Koenig>所以，你事实上真的被赶出退伍军人会馆了吗？

00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:39.690
<v Duke Fightmaster>Yeah, I think that night they picked up the keys. It might have been the next couple nights, but the guy showed up-- and we didn't record it, but he showed up during the show and I gave him the keys. I had to stop the show, and I went out and talked-- he was with his son, it was an old veteran with his son-- and I just said, "Listen, I'm very sorry, but we're broke and we can't pay the rent." [INAUDIBLE].
<v Duke Fightmaster>对，我想那天他们去拿了钥匙。可能是在接下来的几个晚上吧，但那家伙出现了——我们当时没录下来，但他是在演出期间出现的，我把钥匙交给了他。我不得不中断演出，然后走出去谈了谈——他和他的儿子一起来的，那是一个老退伍军人带着他的儿子——我就说：“听着，非常抱歉，但我们破产了，付不起房租。”[听不清]。

00:31:39.690 --> 00:31:41.520
<v Sarah Koenig>How much was the rent?
<v Sarah Koenig>房租是多少？

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:45.050
<v Duke Fightmaster>It was really cheap. It was $30 bucks a night.
<v Duke Fightmaster>真的很便宜。一晚30美元。

00:31:45.050 --> 00:32:08.370
<v Sarah Koenig>After the show got booted from the Veteran's Hall, it moved to a local nightclub, which at first seemed like a step up, but in fact, was the beginning of the end. The space was all wrong, the magic of the bedroom was long gone-- everyone could feel it. So the nightclub shut them down. And still Duke kept going, more or less alone, for another few months until finally, after two and a half years, he had nowhere to go.
<v Sarah Koenig>在演出被退伍军人俱乐部赶出来之后，它搬到了一家当地的夜总会，起初这看起来像是一个升级，但实际上，这是一切走向终结的开始。场地完全不对劲，卧室里那种魔力早已不复存在——每个人都能感觉得到。于是夜总会让他们停业了。杜克依然坚持着，多多少少孤身一人又撑了几个月，直到最后，在两年半之后，他彻底走投无路了。

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:17.820
<v Duke Fightmaster>It was just like the whole roof was falling on my head. I basically just stopped after that.
<v Duke Fightmaster>感觉就像整个屋顶都砸在了我头上。在那之后，我基本上就停下来了。

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:24.040
<v Sarah Koenig>Notice Duke says he stopped. Not that he quit, he stopped. He describes the show as on hiatus.
<v Sarah Koenig>注意杜克说的是他“停下来了”。不是说他放弃了，而是他停下来了。他把演出形容为处于暂停状态。

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:51.510
<v Sarah Koenig>A month or two later Duke got a job, one he says is perfect for him. He works for a company that makes those advertisements you sometimes find hanging from your front doorknob-- they're called door hangers. Duke's job is to fly around the country and take photos of the people whose job it is to hang the hangers on your door. So his job is to make sure they're doing their job. He's a job cop. Which maybe isn't his dream, but his schedule leaves him enough time to do some stand-up and to produce a podcast from his house. Plus, he's making some money.
<v Sarah Koenig>一两个月后，杜克找到了一份工作，一份他认为对完美的适合他的工作。他在一家公司上班，负责制作那种有时你会发现在自家门把手上的广告——它们被称为门把手广告牌。杜克的工作是飞往全国各地，拍摄那些负责把广告牌挂在你门上的人。所以他的工作就是确保他们在工作。他是一个“工作警察”。这或许不是他的梦想，但他的时间安排让他有足够的时间去表演单口喜剧，并在家里制作播客。另外，他还能赚到一些钱。

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:58.330
<v Sarah Koenig>After all he lost during the Duke Fightmaster Show-- the house, car, friends, self-esteem-- he's not sorry he tried it.
<v Sarah Koenig>经历了在“杜克斗士秀”期间失去的一切——房子、汽车、朋友、自尊——他并不后悔自己尝试过。

00:32:58.330 --> 00:33:29.470
<v Duke Fightmaster>If I didn't try to do this talk show, then I would never know, and I would be on my death bed wondering like, "I could have done something." But now that I've done it I know I can't. Going out and saying, "I'm going to be the replacement for Conan O'Brien," it turns out that that's a lot easier said than done. It's not as easy to start a talk show and replace Conan O'Brien as I thought it might have been.
<v Duke Fightmaster>如果我没有尝试去做这个脱口秀，那我永远都不会知道，然后我会在临终前纳闷：“我本可以做点什么的。”但现在我做过了，我知道我做不到。走出去说：“我要成为柯南·奥布莱恩的接班人，”事实证明，说起来容易做起来难。创办一个脱口秀节目并取代柯南·奥布莱恩，并没有我想象的那么容易。

00:33:29.470 --> 00:33:35.140
<v Sarah Koenig>I find that a really funny-- "The lesson I learned is, it's really hard to replace Conan."
<v Sarah Koenig>我觉得这真的很搞笑——“我吸取的教训是，取代柯南真的很难。”

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:39.230
<v Sarah Koenig>[LAUGHTER]
<v Sarah Koenig>[笑声]

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:47.730
<v Sarah Koenig>Because I feel like, well, I could have told you that three years ago. I mean, nobody gets to be Conan O'Brien. Only Conan O'Brien gets to be Conan O'Brien. That's why it's so hard to be Conan O'Brien.
<v Sarah Koenig>因为我觉得，好吧，我三年前就能告诉你了。我的意思是，没有人能成为柯南·奥布莱恩。只有柯南·奥布莱恩才能成为柯南·奥布莱恩。这就是为什么成为柯南·奥布莱恩这么难。

00:33:47.730 --> 00:34:05.190
<v Duke Fightmaster>I think that if you have some outlandish, big deal chasing type of an idea, you have to be in some type of denial to even try it.
<v Duke Fightmaster>我认为，如果你有什么离奇、规模宏大的追求型想法，你甚至必须得有某种程度的否认态度才敢去尝试。

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:46.030
<v Sarah Koenig>Thinking back on the show, how he believed he could do it and how so many other people did too, Duke says it's like he was an alcoholic dad who'd go out drinking and then come home and play with the kids and promise they'd all be going to Disneyland tomorrow. And everybody is so happy and expectant, and then the next morning, the father wakes up and they're not going to Disneyland, and he's forgotten he ever said it. Now that Duke has sobered up, he says really the biggest lesson he learned is that you can't just put your head down and soldier on for two and a half years. At some point, you have to look up. And if you see that, say, your wife, your sidekick, isn't in the audience, you correct your course.
<v Sarah Koenig>回想起这个节目，回想起他曾经坚信自己能做到，以及其他那么多人也曾深信不疑，杜克说，这就好像他是一个酗酒的父亲，出去喝得烂醉，然后回家跟孩子们玩，承诺他们明天都会去迪士尼乐园。每个人都那么快乐，充满期待，然而第二天早上，父亲醒来后发现他们根本不去迪士尼，他也完全忘了自己说过这话。现在杜克已经清醒了，他说他学到的最大教训其实是，你不能只是埋头苦干、硬挺个两年半。在某个时刻，你必须抬起头来看看。如果你发现，比方说，你的妻子、你的搭档并不在观众席里，你就得纠正方向。

00:34:46.030 --> 00:34:52.920
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Koenig is one of the producers of our show. And these are the song stylings of Duke's wife, Leslie, from the show.
<v Ira Glass>萨拉·科尼格是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来是杜克的妻子莱斯利在节目中演唱的歌曲。

00:34:52.920 --> 00:35:14.520
<v Ira Glass>[ACOUSTIC GUITAR AND LESLIE SINGING]
<v Ira Glass>[原声吉他伴奏与莱斯利歌唱]

00:35:14.520 --> 00:36:09.770
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, Car Trek to Star Trek. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>稍后为您播出，从《车迷星际迷航》到《星际迷航》。节目继续，芝加哥公共广播电台与国际公共广播电台联合呈献。

00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:37.080
<v Elna Baker>In my house growing up, there were two pictures of men being hit by beams of light. At the top of the stairs we had a painting of Joseph Smith's first vision. At age 14, he went into the woods looking for answers from God. In the painting, he's fallen to the ground and is looking up through the trees at a pillar of light-- God and Jesus are floating there. According to church history, this is the moment they told Joseph Smith to start a new faith.
<v Elna Baker>在我长大的家里，有两幅关于男人被光束击中的画。在楼梯顶上，我们挂着一幅约瑟夫·史密斯第一次异象的画作。14岁时，他走进树林寻找上帝的答案。在画中，他倒在地上，正透过树木向上凝视着一根光柱——上帝和耶稣正悬浮在那里。根据教会历史，这就是他们告诉约瑟夫·史密斯创立一个新信仰的时刻。

00:36:37.080 --> 00:37:30.030
<v Elna Baker>The other beam of light picture was a poster in my bedroom from the 1993 movie Fire in the Sky. "Alien Abduction," the poster read. November 5, 1975, White Mountains, north eastern Arizona. The layout is remarkably similar to the Joseph Smith painting. Travis Walton, the abducted man, is standing in the woods. A bright blue beam of light shoots from the sky diagonally across the image, striking him in the chest. His body is arched backward and he's suspended a foot above the ground. The beam of light is fuzzy and mysterious, and inside that beam is what made the poster so special. Written with a sharpie were the words, "Elna, don't go towards the light, Travis Walton."
<v Elna Baker>另一幅光柱画是我卧室里的一张海报，来自1993年的电影《烈火天空》。“外星人绑架，”海报上写着。1975年11月5日，亚利桑那州东北部的怀特山脉。其构图与约瑟夫·史密斯的画作惊人地相似。特拉维斯·沃尔顿，那个被绑架的人，正站在树林里。一道明亮的蓝色光柱从天空中斜着穿过画面，击中了他的胸口。他的身体向后反弓着，悬浮在离地一英尺的空中。光柱模糊而神秘，而在这道光柱内部，正是这张海报如此特别的原因。上面用记号笔写着这样一行字：“埃尔娜，不要走向光，特拉维斯·沃尔顿。”

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:46.860
<v Elna Baker>Fire in the Sky is actually based on a true story, and my dad got me the poster because he knew Travis and some of the other guys involved. Among UFO types, it's one of the best known alien abductions.
<v Elna Baker>《烈火天空》实际上是根据真实故事改编的，我爸爸给我弄来了这张海报，因为他认识特拉维斯和其他一些涉事人员。在UFO迷当中，这是最著名的外星人绑架事件之一。

00:37:46.860 --> 00:38:08.370
<v Elna Baker>Travis Walton was part of a logging crew with six other guys. They had been cutting down trees all day and were on their way home. They were all piled into one truck when they say they saw a bright light through the trees. When they got up close, it was a spaceship, hovering. Travis immediately jumped out of the truck to get a closer look. Here's how the movie played that scene.
<v Elna Baker>特拉维斯·沃尔顿是六人伐木队的一员。他们砍了一整天的树，正在回家的路上。他们全都挤在一辆卡车里，这时他们说他们透过树林看到了一道明亮的光。等他们走近一看，是一个悬停着的飞船。特拉维斯立刻跳下卡车去仔细查看。以下是电影中还原那一幕的片段。

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:09.340
<v Movie Character 1>Travis!
<v Movie Character 1>特拉维斯！

00:38:09.340 --> 00:38:11.230
<v Movie Character 2>Travis! You son of a bitch!
<v Movie Character 2>特拉维斯！你这个王八蛋！

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:16.360
<v Movie Character 1>The hell you doing? Get back in the truck [BLEEP]! My God.
<v Movie Character 1>你在搞什么鬼？滚回车里去[哔——]！我的天。

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:39.860
<v Elna Baker>That's when the beam of light struck him in the chest. The impact threw him to the ground. His friends took off, afraid that the UFO would get them next. A quarter mile later they realized the ship wasn't after them and headed back. When they got to the site, Travis was gone. He was missing for five days, and when he returned he claimed he'd been abducted.
<v Elna Baker>就在那时，那道光柱击中了她的胸口。冲击力把他撞倒在地。他的朋友们吓得逃跑了，生怕下一个遭殃的是飞船。四分之一英里后，他们意识到飞船并不是冲着他们来的，于是折了回去。等他们赶到现场时，特拉维斯已经不见了。他失踪了五天，当他回来时，他声称自己被绑架了。

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:56.410
<v Elna Baker>The details are familiar-- aliens with big eyes and big, rounded heads. There's an exam table. But surprise, no probe. And Travis is let go unhurt.
<v Elna Baker>这些细节似曾相识——长着大眼睛和巨大圆脑袋的外星人。还有一个检查台。但惊喜的是，没有探头。而且特拉维斯被毫发无伤地放了回来。

00:38:56.410 --> 00:39:15.020
<v Elna Baker>My dad knew these guys because he was from the same town as Travis and all six witnesses-- Snowflake, Arizona. It's a small Mormon town named for the two pioneers who founded it, Erastus Snow and William Jordan Flake. Almost everyone in town is either a Snow or a Flake, and Mormon.
<v Elna Baker>我爸爸认识这些人，因为他跟特拉维斯以及所有六个见证人都来自同一个小镇——亚利桑那州的斯诺flake镇。这是一个为了纪念建立它的两位先驱埃拉斯特斯·斯诺和威廉·约旦·弗莱克而命名的莫尔门教小镇。镇上的几乎每个人不是姓斯诺就是姓弗莱克，并且都是莫尔门教徒。

00:39:15.020 --> 00:39:25.200
<v Elna Baker>A month ago, I found myself driving north from Phoenix towards the White Mountains with my soon-to-be be brother-in-law Josh Dickens and This American Life producer Jonathan Menjivar.
<v Elna Baker>一个月前，我发现自己正开着车，从菲尼克斯一路向北驶向怀特山脉，同行的还有我即将成为连襟的乔什·迪肯斯以及《这美国生活》的制作人乔纳森·门吉瓦尔。

00:39:25.200 --> 00:39:27.670
<v Jonathan Menjivar>So what the hell are we doing right now?
<v Jonathan Menjivar>所以我们现在到底在干什么？

00:39:27.670 --> 00:39:49.530
<v Elna Baker>Right now we are driving to the mountains to meet Ken Peterson and Mike Rogers, who were both witnesses to Travis Walton's abduction by aliens in 1975-- 35 years ago today. And we're driving up there to camp overnight on the site of the abduction with Ken.
<v Elna Baker>我们现在正开车去山里，去见肯·彼得森和迈克·罗杰斯，他们都是1975年特拉维斯·沃尔顿被外星人绑架事件的见证人——正好是35年前的今天。我们要开车去那里，和肯一起在绑架发生的地点露营过夜。

00:39:49.530 --> 00:40:16.280
<v Elna Baker>Ken has camped out on the site of the abduction before. Three times. Of all the witnesses that night, he's the only one who still fixates on it. He feels like he missed his chance to connect with these other beings, to be taken on board the space craft the way Travis had been. In the years immediately after the event, Ken assumed the aliens would come back and find them again, that there was a reason they'd reached out to him and his friends, and he wants a re-do.
<v Elna Baker>肯以前曾在绑架发生的地点露营过。三次。在当晚所有的见证人中，他是唯一一个仍然对这件事念念不忘的人。他觉得自己错失了与这些其他生命沟通的机会，错失了像特拉维斯那样被带上太空船的机会。在事件发生后的几年里，肯一直以为外星人会回来再次找到他们，觉得他们主动联系他和他的朋友是有原因的，他想重新来一次。

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:33.230
<v Elna Baker>This interests me for reasons that are hard to talk about quickly and glibly on the radio, but I'd had my own encounter in the woods, years ago. When I was 14, I went on a church hiking trip, and they told us all to go into the woods like Joseph Smith did and pray.
<v Elna Baker>这件事吸引我的原因，很难在电台里三言两语轻率地讲清楚，但多年前，我也曾在树林里有过自己的遭遇。在我14岁的时候，我参加了一次教会的徒步旅行，他们告诉我们所有人像约瑟夫·史密斯那样走进树林里祈祷。

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:58.520
<v Elna Baker>I found a quite clearing, knelt down, and asked a kind of mushy combination of, "Is there really a God," and "If there is, am I supposed to be a Mormon?" And I waited. And then the sun came through the clouds and warm light hit my face. I felt like someone was wrapping their arms around me and hugging me. My body rocked back and forth, and I knew it wasn't me who was doing it.
<v Elna Baker>我找到了一片安静的空地，单膝跪下，带着一种模棱两可的混合心愿祈祷：“到底有没有上帝？”以及“如果有的话，我是不是应该成为一名莫尔门教徒？”然后我等待着。接着，阳光穿透云层，温暖的光线照在我的脸上。我感觉好像有人正伸出双臂环抱着我。我的身体前后摇晃着，我知道这不是我自己控制的。

00:40:58.520 --> 00:41:30.300
<v Elna Baker>But over the last two years, I've lost my faith. The scale of this is hard to communicate. It affects everything. My friends, what I think about when I'm walking down the street, what I use for comfort. Not being able to pray, not having God as a constant companion, I feel like my best friend has died. And when I look back on that moment and I had in the woods, I wonder if it was all in my head. And I've wished it could happen again. I've wished that so many times.
<v Elna Baker>但在过去两年里，我失去了信仰。这种程度的冲击很难用言语表达。它影响了一切。我的朋友、我走在街上时的所思所想、我用来寻求慰藉的东西。无法再祈祷，没有了上帝作为常伴身侧的同伴，我感觉自己最好的朋友去世了。当我回想起当年在树林里的那个时刻，我禁不住怀疑这一切是否只是我的臆想。我多么希望这一切能再次发生。我有过无数次这样的祈祷。

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:46.650
<v Elna Baker>And I know it's ridiculous to compare knowing God to seeing UFOs-- they're nothing alike. But because of my experience in the woods, I thought I understood Ken's yearning for a second encounter. That's why I wanted to talk to him.
<v Elna Baker>我也知道，把认识上帝和看到UFO作比较是荒谬的——它们毫无共同之处。但正因为我在树林里的经历，我觉得自己能理解肯对第二次遭遇的渴望。这就是我想跟他聊聊的原因。

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:57.430
<v Jonathan Menjivar>So do you have any expectations for what's going to happen tonight? I mean, I guess, why do you want to talk to Ken about these things?
<v Jonathan Menjivar>那你对今晚会发生什么有什么期待吗？我的意思是，我想知道，你为什么想和肯聊聊这些事情？

00:41:57.430 --> 00:42:24.310
<v Elna Baker>I want to hear what he saw, first of all. And I want to know how he's been able to, in spite of obstacles and in spite of-- it's not a convenient thing to believe, everyone in Snowflake thinks he's crazy and makes fun of him for believing these things-- how and why it matters to him to believe.
<v Elna Baker>首先，我想听听他看到了什么。而且我想知道，尽管面临种种阻碍和非议——相信这些并不是一件多省心的事，斯诺flake镇上的所有人都觉得他疯了，并拿这些事取笑他——他是如何以及为什么认为去相信这些依然对得很重要。

00:42:24.310 --> 00:42:30.850
<v Jonathan Menjivar>But do you feel like you're going to get any answers about your crisis of faith talking to Ken?
<v Jonathan Menjivar>但你觉得和肯谈话能为你关于信仰危机的疑问找到答案吗？

00:42:30.850 --> 00:42:53.730
<v Elna Baker>I don't know. I think I've been avoiding talking about any of the stuff we've been talking about, admitting that I still want to believe. But I think this is an experience where I've allowed myself to think about it. And I was hoping to at least come to some sense of peace of mind.
<v Elna Baker>我不知道。我想我一直都在回避谈论我们刚才聊过的任何事情，回避承认我内心深处依然想要相信。但我认为这是一次允许自己去思考这个问题的经历。我曾希望能借此至少获得某种心灵上的平静。

00:42:53.730 --> 00:43:27.460
<v Elna Baker>For decades, Travis and the others have lived among people who doubt them. Their story made international news, and they passed polygraph tests, but no one in Snowflake believed them. My Uncle Sank was the sheriff in charge of the investigation. James Garner plays him in the film, and he led the skeptics. I remember when I was a kid, people made fun of Travis. There was even a poem published in the local paper whose gist was, "The aliens wanted a perfect human specimen, but they got Travis Walton instead."
<v Elna Baker>几十年来，特拉维斯和其他人一直生活在充满怀疑的人群中。他们的故事上了国际新闻，他们也通过了测谎仪测试，但斯诺flake镇没有一个人相信他们。我的叔叔桑克是负责调查的警长。詹姆斯·加纳在电影中扮演他，他领导了怀疑论者阵营。我记得当我还是个孩子的时候，人们经常取笑特拉维斯。当地报纸上甚至还发表过一首诗，其大意是：“外星人们想要一个完美的人类标本，但他们最后却带走了特拉维斯·沃尔顿。”

00:43:27.460 --> 00:43:31.820
<v Josh Dickens>It's such a cool underdog story, I guess.
<v Josh Dickens>我想，这真是一个酷极了的草根逆袭的故事。

00:43:31.820 --> 00:43:40.470
<v Elna Baker>My brother-in-law to be, Josh, is along for the ride because Fire in the Sky is his favorite movie. When he found out that my dad knew Travis Walton, he lost it.
<v Elna Baker>我未来的连襟乔什也跟着一起来了，因为《烈火天空》是他最喜欢的电影。当他发现我爸爸认识特拉维斯·沃尔顿的时候，他激动得快疯了。

00:43:40.470 --> 00:44:00.490
<v Josh Dickens>I immediately called one of my friends, who-- we're like total Fire in the Sky nerds. And we were flipping out together on the phone, and how exciting it was. And I'm sending him an invitation to our wedding.
<v Josh Dickens>我立刻给我的一个朋友打了电话，我们俩——我们就像是彻头彻尾的《烈火天空》超级书呆子。我们在电话里激动得语无伦次，讨论着这有多让人兴奋。我甚至还给他发了一张我们婚礼的请柬。

00:44:00.490 --> 00:44:19.450
<v Elna Baker>We meet up with our two witnesses, Ken and Mike. And Jonathan and I ride up with them. Josh is in the four wheel drive behind us. Mike is burly with a wavy mullet. Ken looks older that his 60 years. Mike's behind the wheel like he was on the day of the abduction. Here's Ken, who's sitting next to him.
<v Elna Baker>我们和两位见证人肯和迈克汇合了。乔纳森和我坐他们的车上去。乔什开着四驱车跟在我们后面。迈克身材魁梧，留着波浪形的发型。肯看起来比他60岁的实际年龄还要苍老。迈克坐在驾驶座上，就像在被绑架那天一样。这位是坐在他旁边的肯。

00:44:19.450 --> 00:44:28.890
<v Ken Peterson>I'm kind of excited. This is my first time out there on the anniversary date. Mike's, also.
<v Ken Peterson>我有点激动。这是我第一次在周年纪念日这一天来到这里。迈克也是。

00:44:28.890 --> 00:44:54.470
<v Elna Baker>We turn onto small, bumpy dirt roads, and as we approach the peak of the mountains, the forest looks bleak. Eight years ago, a fire, one of the worst in Arizona's history, swept through the mountains. It burned for 20 days and wiped out almost half a million acres leaving mostly empty space, broken up by little burnt spires like stumps jutting out of a swamp, only without the swamp.
<v Elna Baker>我们拐上了一条颠簸的小土路，随着我们接近山顶，森林显得一片荒凉。八年前，一场大火——亚利桑那州历史上最严重的大火之一——席卷了群山。大火燃烧了20天，吞噬了近五十万英亩的土地，留下的几乎是一片空旷，中间点缀着一些烧焦的尖顶，就像从沼泽里突出来的树桩，只可惜这里没有沼泽。

00:44:54.470 --> 00:45:09.040
<v Elna Baker>The fire makes it almost impossible for Ken and Mike to recognize the site, and we got lost. We get out of the car several times, trying old fire roads covered with pine needles and dirt and fallen logs. Ken fishes for anything that might be a clue.
<v Elna Baker>这场大火让肯和迈克几乎无法辨认出原来的地点，我们迷路了。我们好几次下车，试图寻找那些铺满松针、泥土和倒伏圆木的旧防火路。肯四处搜寻着任何可能是线索的东西。

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:13.150
<v Ken Peterson>Seems like I remember this leaning pine tree over here.
<v Ken Peterson>我记得这附近好像有一棵倾斜的松树。

00:45:13.150 --> 00:45:21.830
<v Elna Baker>Up close, those burnt spires look like Martian cacti. Finally, they see a pile of stones they left as a marker years ago.
<v Elna Baker>近看之下，那些烧焦的尖顶看起来就像火星上的仙人掌。终于，他们看到了一堆多年前作为标记留下的石头。

00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:23.900
<v Ken Peterson>Well, that's the road, right?
<v Ken Peterson>好吧，这就是那条路，对吧？

00:45:23.900 --> 00:45:24.820
<v Mike Rogers>Yeah, down ahead.
<v Mike Rogers>对，在前面。

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:25.910
<v Ken Peterson>Down there.
<v Ken Peterson>就在下面。

00:45:25.910 --> 00:45:28.320
<v Mike Rogers>The UFO would've been up here close to the top of the ridge.
<v Mike Rogers>UFO应该就在这里靠近山脊顶部的地方。

00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:35.870
<v Elna Baker>I thought it was just, like, straight woods. I didn't realize there was a ridge and a drop-off. It's pretty up here.
<v Elna Baker>我还以为这里就是一片普通的树林呢。我没意识到这里有山脊和悬崖。这上面风景真不错。

00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:48.800
<v Elna Baker>From the ridge where they say the UFO hovered, you can look out over the whole valley below. Ken started telling us about that night. When they first saw the light through the trees, they thought it might be hunters or maybe a small forest fire.
<v Elna Baker>从他们说UFO悬停的山脊望出去，你可以俯瞰下面的整个山谷。肯开始给我们讲述那个晚上的情景。当他们第一次透过树林看到那束光时，他们以为可能是猎人，或者是一场小型森林大火。

00:45:48.800 --> 00:46:06.840
<v Ken Peterson>We got right to this spot, and there it was. It was probably about the size of two vans. Perfect saucer-shaped. Kind of a glow coming out of it, real soft light just lighting up the area around it.
<v Ken Peterson>我们直接来到了这个地方，它就在那儿。它大概有两辆面包车那么大。完美的飞碟形状。散发着一种柔和的光芒，正柔和地照亮着周边的区域。

00:46:06.840 --> 00:46:26.790
<v Ken Peterson>Travis immediately got out of the truck and then all of a sudden I see the saucer start to wobble a little bit. A flash of light come out, last thing I saw was Travis's arms outstretched.
<v Ken Peterson>特拉维斯立刻从卡车里跳了下来，紧接着我突然看到飞碟开始微微晃动。一道闪光射出，我看到的最后一个画面是特拉维斯伸开双臂。

00:46:26.790 --> 00:46:31.600
<v Elna Baker>As you'd imagine, Josh couldn't be more excited. Even Ken notices.
<v Elna Baker>正如你所想象的那样，乔什兴奋得无以复加。连肯都注意到了。

00:46:31.600 --> 00:46:35.370
<v Ken Peterson>That's nice to see you come along, be part of it.
<v Ken Peterson>很高兴看到你能一起来，成为其中的一部分。

00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:46.580
<v Josh Dickens>Yeah, well I didn't want to sound like a bumbling fan or anything, but it really has always been a huge, I guess you could say, dream.
<v Josh Dickens>是啊，我本来不想表现得像个笨手笨脚的狂热粉丝什么的，但这真的可以说是长久以来我最大的一个梦想吧。

00:46:46.580 --> 00:46:57.840
<v Elna Baker>We watch the sun go down behind the mountains. Mike hadn't planned on staying the night. He said he had a nice home with a warm bed where he didn't have to worry about coyotes, bears or aliens.
<v Elna Baker>我们看着太阳在群山背后落下。迈克没打算过夜。他说他家里有一张温暖舒适的床，不用担心什么郊狼、熊或者外星人。

00:46:57.840 --> 00:46:59.200
<v Mike Rogers>Well, you take care.
<v Mike Rogers>好了，保重。

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:02.740
<v Ken Peterson>All right. Thanks for coming up for the anniversary.
<v Ken Peterson>好嘞。谢谢你们为纪念日特意上来一趟。

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:05.400
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, happy 35th, guys.
<v Elna Baker>对啊，35周年快乐，伙计们。

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:09.080
<v Ken Peterson>I said, "Happy Anniversary, Sweetheart," to him this morning.
<v Ken Peterson>我今天早上对他说：“结婚纪念日快乐，亲爱的。”

00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:18.440
<v Mike Rogers>Like an old unmarried couple.
<v Mike Rogers>就像一对老夫老妻。

00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:46.950
<v Elna Baker>We found a flat spot to set up camp. Inside the tent, we all huddled around a little propane heater. Ken started telling us a little bit about himself. Prior to this trip, all I knew was that he was divorced, had kids, lived alone in a trailer, and that I had to call him at his sister's because he couldn't afford to use his cell phone minutes. He told us he spends two or three days a week in the library using the internet to research his UFO theories, which he has time for because he's pretty much retired now.
<v Elna Baker>我们找了一处平地扎营。在帐篷里，大家挤在一个小型丙烷加热器周围。肯开始向我们讲一些他自己的事。在这趟旅行之前，我只知道他离过婚，有孩子，独自住在拖车里，而且我得打给他姐姐才能联系到他，因为他买不起手机通话时长。他告诉我们，他每周有两三天会在图书馆用网络研究他的UFO理论，他有时间这么做是因为他现在基本上已经退休了。

00:47:46.950 --> 00:48:12.770
<v Ken Peterson>And I tell everybody-- I have four sons-- I tell people that they put me out to pasture. Just live by myself, and I'm just totally obsessed. Everybody else realized that I'm obsessed with the UFO encounter.
<v Ken Peterson>我告诉所有人——我有四个儿子——我告诉人们他们把我闲置了。独自生活，我完全着迷了。其他人意识到我对UFO的遭遇着迷了。

00:48:12.770 --> 00:48:17.020
<v Elna Baker>Do you wish it had been you?
<v Elna Baker>你希望当时经历这一切的是你吗？

00:48:17.020 --> 00:48:40.140
<v Ken Peterson>Yeah. Yeah, I wished I would have went over there with him. Right now, with 20/20 hindsight, and knowing that nothing that terrible happened to him, it would have been quite an experience to have gone for the ride.
<v Ken Peterson>对。对，我希望当时能和他一起去那边。现在，有了后见之明，而且知道他没有遭遇什么太可怕的事，能去经历那一遭会是一段相当美妙的体验。

00:48:40.140 --> 00:48:57.840
<v Elna Baker>During the time after when you sort of hoped that it might happen again, or wondered if it would happen again, and then more time passed and it hadn't happened, did you ever lose faith in it? Feel sincerely doubtful?
<v Elna Baker>在那之后的一段时间里，你是否曾希望这种事会再次发生，或者好奇它是否会再次发生，然后随着时间推移，这种事并没有再次发生，你是否曾对它失去信念？感到由衷的怀疑？

00:48:57.840 --> 00:49:16.590
<v Ken Peterson>Never about the incident. I've always believed that Travis was taken. It's been a cross to bear with family, and Mormons in general.
<v Ken Peterson>对那次事件从不怀疑。我一直相信特拉维斯被带走了。对于家庭和整体的摩门教徒来说，这一直是一个沉重的负担。

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:18.260
<v Elna Baker>What do Mormons say?
<v Elna Baker>摩门教徒们是怎么说的？

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:50.300
<v Ken Peterson>Well, they don't seem to be too interested in ETs or UFOs. With my sons, for a long time I was telling them about these things I was experiencing, and they got pretty tired of it. And my sister, she's totally turned off by it. That's the lonely part of this, is nobody will hear me out on it.
<v Ken Peterson>嗯，他们似乎对天外来客或UFO不太感兴趣。对于我的儿子们，很长一段时间我都在向他们讲述我所经历的这些事，他们对此感到非常厌倦。还有我姐姐，她对此完全无感。这就是痛苦孤独的部分，没有人肯听我倾诉。

00:49:50.300 --> 00:50:00.370
<v Elna Baker>Earlier, I'd asked Mike how he managed to hold on to his version of the events in the face of so much opposition. Did he ever have doubts?
<v Elna Baker>早些时候，我曾问过迈克，在面对如此多的反对声音时，他是如何坚守自己对事件版本的信念的。他曾经有过怀疑吗？

00:50:00.370 --> 00:50:39.180
<v Mike Rogers>I'm very skeptical of anything and everything else. I even have certain skepticisms about this. I would have even doubted my own self. If something like this would have happened to me by myself, or even with one other person and that person was gone or abducted or whatever, I would doubt my own side. I think over a few years I would have just decided it was some kind of a dream or some kind of an odd occurrence that has no answer, and I left it at that. But there were seven of us out here, so it made it pretty easy to rely on the other guys for complete and continuous memory.
<v Mike Rogers>我对其他任何及所有事物都持高度怀疑态度。我甚至对这件事本身也抱有某些怀疑。我甚至会怀疑我自己。如果这样的事发生在我孤身一人时，或者即使还有另一个人，而那个人离开了、被绑架了什么的，我会怀疑我自己的记忆。我想几年下来，我可能只会认为那是一种梦境，或者某种无法解答的离奇事件，然后就这样抛在脑后了。但我们这里有七个人，所以这让我们很容易依靠其他人来获得完整且持续的共同记忆。

00:50:39.180 --> 00:51:02.190
<v Elna Baker>They have each other, so they don't doubt. When Mike said this, I thought about how the Book of Mormon begins with a document, the Testimony of the Three Witnesses, each claiming that they saw the golden plates Joseph Smith translated into scripture. It's supposed to fulfill this prophecy from the Bible that says, "In the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word be established."
<v Elna Baker>他们拥有彼此，所以他们不会怀疑。当迈克这么说时，我想到了《摩门教经》是如何以一份文件开头的，即《三位见证人的证言》，每个人都声称他们看到了约瑟·斯密翻译成经文的金页片。这被认为是应验了圣经中的预言：“凭两三人的口作见证，句句都要定准。”

00:51:02.190 --> 00:51:15.240
<v Elna Baker>Basically, God made sure Joseph showed at least three people the plates so they could sustain each other's faith, and so that other people would believe them. That's what I don't have-- other witnesses.
<v Elna Baker>基本上，上帝确保约瑟向至少三个人展示了页片，这样他们就可以相互支撑彼此的信念，并让其他人相信他们。这就是我所没有的——其他的见证人。

00:51:15.240 --> 00:51:33.020
<v Elna Baker>Ever since I moved to New York, fewer and fewer of my friends are Mormon. Now I can count them on one hand. We don't want to believe our faith is contingent on who we hang out with, but there you are.
<v Elna Baker>自从我搬到纽约以来，我的摩门教朋友越来越少。现在我一只手就能数过来。我们不想相信我们的信仰取决于我们和谁在一起，但事实就是如此。

00:51:33.020 --> 00:51:48.730
<v Elna Baker>As it gets later, things take an unexpected turn. Ken tells me it's easy to hold on to what he believes about their encounter in the woods because he sees proof all the time. Signs and coincidences going back to that night they encountered the UFO.
<v Elna Baker>随着时间越来越晚，事情发生了意想不到的转折。肯告诉我，他很容易坚守自己对他们在树林里遭遇UFO的信仰，因为他无时无刻不在看到证据。那些追溯到他们遭遇UFO那个夜晚的征兆和巧合。

00:51:48.730 --> 00:51:59.410
<v Ken Peterson>I believe that it was intentional, that it was a display of the symbology of the phoenix, mythological phoenix.
<v Ken Peterson>我相信那是故意的，那是凤凰神话中凤凰象征意义的一次展示。

00:51:59.410 --> 00:52:03.880
<v Elna Baker>The phoenix, the mythological bird that died and was reborn.
<v Elna Baker>凤凰，那只死后重生的神话鸟类。

00:52:03.880 --> 00:52:24.360
<v Ken Peterson>You know, death and rebirth. And, you know, we're fairly close to Phoenix. Three of the guys were on our crew had just come up from Phoenix. And, of course, afterwards with all the media coverage and all the Phoenix TV stations there.
<v Ken Peterson>你知道的，死亡与重生。而且，你知道的，我们离凤凰城很近。我们船员中的三个人刚从凤凰城上来。当然，在那之后还有所有的媒体报道以及那里的所有凤凰城电视台。

00:52:24.360 --> 00:52:39.720
<v Elna Baker>He keeps unspooling these associations with the word phoenix. "You'll think this is really crazy," he says more than once, and the thought does occur to me. If he could just figure out the meaning of these signs, he says, he'd understand the aliens' plan.
<v Elna Baker>他不断地将这些联想与“凤凰”这个词串联起来。“你会觉得这真的很疯狂，”他不止一次地说，而这个想法也确实闪现在我的脑海里。他说，如果他能弄懂这些征兆的含义，他就能理解外星人的计划。

00:52:39.720 --> 00:53:02.810
<v Elna Baker>"Lately," he tells us, "all signs point to the actors Joaquin and River Phoenix. One is living, one is dead," he says, "so there's death and rebirth again, like the Phoenix." He bought some bread from a woman whose last name was river, like River Phoenix. She knew someone who worked on the movie Signs, which starred Joaquin Phoenix, and this woman, River, turned out to be Puerto Rican.
<v Elna Baker>“最近，”他告诉我们，“所有的征兆都指向演员华金·菲尼克斯和瑞凡·菲尼克斯兄弟。一个活着，一个死了，”他说，“所以这里又有了死亡和重生，就像凤凰一样。”他从一个姓“河”（River，如瑞凡·菲尼克斯的名字）的女人那里买了一些面包。她认识一个曾参与电影《天兆》工作的人，该电影由华金·菲尼克斯主演，而这个名叫瑞凡的女人原来是波多黎各人。

00:53:02.810 --> 00:53:17.800
<v Ken Peterson>She was born in San Juan, Puerto Rico. That's where Joaquin Phoenix was born. She was telling me this on Joaquin Phoenix's birthday, right?
<v Ken Peterson>她出生在波多黎各的圣胡安。那是华金·菲尼克斯出生的地方。她是在华金·菲尼克斯的生日那天把这事告诉我的，对吧？

00:53:17.800 --> 00:53:36.790
<v Elna Baker>Ken told us that he sees these kinds of connections all the time now, in the smallest things. Like after he bought that loaf of bread, I called, wanting to interview him, and my last name is Baker. Bread. Baker. The store next to the stand where he bought the bread was called Big 5 Sporting Goods.
<v Elna Baker>肯告诉我们，他现在无时无刻不在最微小的事情中看到这种联系。就像在他买完那条面包后，我打电话想要采访他，而我姓贝克（Baker）。面包。贝克。他买面包的摊位旁边的商店叫“大五体育用品”（Big 5 Sporting Goods）。

00:53:36.790 --> 00:53:45.900
<v Ken Peterson>So then I think, "what's this Big 5 about?" And I see on the Big 5 that there's a sign there that says, "Shooting star."
<v Ken Peterson>然后我想，“这个‘大五’是什么意思？”接着我看到“大五”上面有一个牌子，上面写着：“流星”。

00:53:45.900 --> 00:54:07.500
<v Elna Baker>The way he spoke felt familiar. I used to notice signs all the time. Everything had a meaning, and it all added up to something. I'd be broke and alone and look down and see a ladybug on my should and think, "See? Someone is watching out for me." Ken was just more extreme about it, like his thing about bread and Big 5.
<v Elna Baker>他说话的方式让我感到熟悉。我过去也总是注意到各种征兆。每件事都有含义，而且它们都指向某种结果。我当时身无分文又孤身一人，低头看到肩膀上有一只瓢虫，就会想：“看？有人在守护着我。”肯只是在这方面更极端一些，就像他对面包和大五体育用品的执念一样。

00:54:07.500 --> 00:54:14.290
<v Ken Peterson>And I see on the Big 5 that there's a sign there that says, "Shooting star."
<v Ken Peterson>接着我看到“大五”上面有一个牌子，上面写着：“流星”。

00:54:14.290 --> 00:54:42.070
<v Elna Baker>It's almost like-- Now I'm playing the same game. The last big moment that I feel like was a spiritual experience that I had was probably three years ago, maybe four years ago, where I felt like it was getting so hard to believe for me. And I just was like, "You know, I want a sign again like the one I had when I was young. And I just want you tell me that you're there, God."
<v Elna Baker>这几乎就像——现在我也在玩同样的游戏。上一个让我感觉是灵性体验的重要时刻，可能是在三年前，也许是四年前，当时我觉得对信仰的信念变得越来越难维持。然后我就想：“你知道的，我还想要一个像我年轻时经历过的那样的征兆。我只希望你告诉我你就在那里，上帝。”

00:54:42.070 --> 00:55:09.390
<v Elna Baker>And I knelt down and I prayed and I asked this. And then I looked up at the sky and I was like, "The sky? That's the sign?" Like, anyone can see this. This isn't a sign. You just see a few stars. It's New York-- you see, like, maybe five stars. And just as I was saying, "This isn't anything, this is just what's always there," one of the stars shot across the sky. And it was biggest shooting star I'd ever seen.
<v Elna Baker>然后我跪下来祈祷并提出了这个请求。接着我抬头看着天空，心想：“天空？这就是征兆？”就像，任何人都能看到这个。这根本不是征兆。你只能看到几颗星星。这里是纽约——你大概只能看到，比如五颗星星。就在我正说着“这算什么，这不过是一直都在那里的东西”时，一颗星星划过夜空。那是我见过的最大的流星。

00:55:09.390 --> 00:55:56.030
<v Elna Baker>I still don't know what to think of that moment. It was shocking. But as soon as it happened, I did the thing I do now-- I started questioning. Was that meant for me? Or did I just happen to look up at the exact moment when a star shot across they sky? I had forgotten about that moment until I shared it with Ken, and it made me think, "Well, that's an even bigger sign that what I experienced in the woods as a teenager, so really, I have had signs since then." And that's when I realized I don't just want a sign, I want to be myself at 14 again-- the kind of person who believes in signs.
<v Elna Baker>我仍然不知道该如何看待那一刻。太震撼了。但就在它发生的那一刻，我做出了我现在常做的事——我开始质疑。那是专门给我的吗？还是我恰好在星星划过天空的精确瞬间抬头了？我差点把那一刻忘得精光，直到我把它分享给肯，这让我觉得，“好吧，这甚至比我十几岁时在树林里经历的征兆还要宏大，所以实际上，从那以后我确实经历过征兆。”就在那时我意识到，我不只是想要一个征兆，我想重新变成14岁的自己——那种相信征兆的人。

00:55:56.030 --> 00:56:03.660
<v Ira Glass>Elna Baker. She's the author of a memoir called, New York Regional Mormon Singles Halloween Dance.
<v Ira Glass>埃尔娜·贝克。她是回忆录《纽约地区摩门教单身万圣节舞会》的作者。

00:56:03.660 --> 00:56:11.660
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC  - "MY FLYING SAUCER" BY BILLY BRAGG AND WILCO]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 比利·布拉格与威尔科乐队的《我的飞碟》]

00:57:12.160 --> 00:57:21.250
<v Rod Blagojevich>I've got this thing, and it's [BLEEP] golden. And I'm just not giving it up for [BLEEP] nothing.
<v Rod Blagojevich>我拥有这件东西，它他妈的价值连城。我绝不会为任何他妈的屁事放弃它。

00:57:21.250 --> 00:57:26.250
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。
